#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206} :activity/id #uuid "4dd7be6e-e9a7-53a7-9076-0bf7c12d09ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048919 :topic/id #uuid "cc7fb2dc-62dc-56f4-976f-30935824115a" :topic/math-academy-id 344} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065206 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065396 :tutorial/content "When a function $y=y(x)$ contains complex products, quotients, or radicals, we can use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative $y'(x).$ \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\ny = x^2 \\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\n \n\nOur goal is to find $y'(x).$ To do this, we will use the product rule for logarithms, namely\n\n$$\n\\ln(a b)= \\ln a + \\ln b.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe differentiate our function by following four steps.\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the product and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Next, we differentiate both sides of our equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{′}+(x)^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}+1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Then, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for $y =x^2\\cdot 2^x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}(\\frac{2}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =x\\cdot {2}^{x+1}+{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1895a7f3-5ba4-5a31-85b3-6a7b050b307c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6088 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "caef414d-4f2a-5504-9b92-39a03d2f3c8f" :step/math-academy-id 37035 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065207 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064309 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064146 :question/id #uuid "c24da7ef-0070-5aae-bade-c82ea56476ba" :question/math-academy-id "e-9845"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7613eee3-a281-57cb-bc47-3b1594920bd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050375}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506517} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506543} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506581}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Product"} :step/id #uuid "b6a516b4-d0c4-5ec7-8f7e-12edd0e85c51" :step/math-academy-id 37036 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064310 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064147 :question/id #uuid "7db11c8c-344b-5d87-a8ae-3b006e5bd92b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9846"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71dd9228-4b05-54d9-aeb2-3bdabf89e0e9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050542}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534090}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "57a60273-bc58-5aff-8dcd-d7139782aa2a" :step/math-academy-id 37037 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064311 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064148 :question/id #uuid "e0cec84d-96cf-5fed-ba05-a5cfc57841a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9847"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6005f9a-3eba-5f8d-909d-894d9cd24d12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050894}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506518} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534067}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Radical Expression"} :step/id #uuid "8850f22e-33ee-545a-8f5e-4dc37bda3b30" :step/math-academy-id 37038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065210 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065397 :tutorial/content "In cases where we wish to differentiate a quotient of functions\n\n$$\ny =  f(x) = \\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)},\n$$\n\nwe can apply the quotient rule for logarithms: \n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\n$$\n\n \n\nFor example, let's compute $y',$ where the function $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e^{x^2}}{(1-2x)^2(x-1)^3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{x}^{2}})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-2x)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((x-1)^{3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & ={x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-2x)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2x-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-1} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute our expression for $y,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}(2x+\\frac{4}{1-2x}-\\frac{3}{x-1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{2}(x-1)^{3}}(\\frac{2x(1-2x)(x-1)+4(x-1)-3(1-2x)}{(1-2x)(x-1)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(2x(1-2x)(x-1)+4(x-1)-3(1-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+8x-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x+7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{{x}^{2}}(4{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-8x+7)}{(1-2x)^{3}(x-1)^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8cf6e625-669a-5fda-b756-e1ce091eaa93" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6090 :tutorial/title "Using Logarithms to Differentiate a Quotient"} :step/id #uuid "2141558f-a6d2-515d-902a-620581d72401" :step/math-academy-id 37041 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065208}}] :activity/title "Further Logarithmic Differentiation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064435}]}
